        <h2 id="how" class="h4 mt-5">1. How a slip is read</h2>
        <p>Every photo is read in two AI calls. Both start with the <strong>shared system prompt</strong>, which holds the rules for every answer.</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Step 1, detect:</strong> the <strong>type detection prompt</strong> asks which type of document the photo shows (fuel, weighbridge, odometer, receipt or one of the company's extra types), or "other".</li>
            <li><strong>Step 2, read:</strong> the chosen <strong>capture type's prompt</strong> asks for that type's fields and gets them back as json.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>If step 1 says "other" or is not sure, the slip goes to review and the reviewer picks the type. The AI only fills in the data: TransLog's own checks and the reviewer decide whether it is right.</p>
        <div class="alert alert-info small">Since Part 5a2b1 you can try any photo on the <a href="/sysadmin/aitest">AI test</a> page. Slips that drivers and the office send are read automatically from Part 5a2b2; until then they wait as "Waiting to be read".</div>

        <h2 id="kinds" class="h4 mt-5">2. The three kinds of entry</h2>
        <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table small align-middle">
                <thead><tr><th>Kind</th><th>Key</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Shared system prompt</td><td><code>system</code></td><td>Rules for every call: read only what is printed, null when missing, S is not 8, dates as YYYY-MM-DD, ignore camera stamps, one json answer.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Type detection</td><td><code>detect</code></td><td>Step 1. Lists the company's types through <code>{types}</code> and asks for one key.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Capture type</td><td><code>fuel</code>, <code>weighbridge</code>, <code>odometer</code>, <code>receipt</code>, or your own</td><td>Step 2. A description (read by step 1), a field table and the reading prompt with hints.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p>The six built-in entries are added when the site starts if they are missing. Your changes are never overwritten. The <strong>key</strong> never changes after an entry is made; "other" cannot be used as a key.</p>

        <h2 id="placeholders" class="h4 mt-5">3. {types} and {fields}</h2>
        <p>You can write every prompt in plain words: TransLog adds the list of types, the list of fields and the json instruction by itself. If you want them somewhere in the middle of your text, put these two words where they should go:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><code>{types}</code> (detection prompt; added at the end when missing) becomes one line per active type for that company, made from its key and description, plus "other":
                <pre class="tl-card small mt-2 mb-2">- fuel: A fuel or diesel slip from a filling station …
- receipt: Any other till slip, invoice or receipt …
- other: anything else, or no document in the photo</pre>
                So the <strong>description</strong> of a type is what step 1 uses to tell types apart. Write it as "a photo is this type when …".
            </li>
            <li><code>{fields}</code> (capture types; added at the end when missing) becomes the field list from the field table plus an example answer in json:
                <pre class="tl-card small mt-2 mb-2">Fields to read:
- litres (number, l): Litres. Usually printed; look carefully before giving null
- vendor (text as printed): Filling station
…
Answer in json exactly in this shape …
{"fields":{"litres":{"value":123.4,"confidence":"high"}, …}}</pre>
                Because the list comes from the field table, the prompt and the fields can never disagree. Write only the <strong>hints</strong> yourself.
            </li>
        </ul>
        <p>To see a prompt exactly as the AI gets it: open a type and fold out <em>Step 2 prompt as the AI gets it</em>, or use <a href="#effective">Effective for company</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="fields" class="h4 mt-5">4. The field table</h2>
        <div class="table-responsive">
            <table class="table small align-middle">
                <thead><tr><th>Column</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr><td>Key</td><td>Under <strong>More</strong>. The name in the json answer and in reports, made from the label when you leave it empty ("Slip number" → <code>slip_number</code>). Each key only once per type. Changing a key later starts a new field; old slips keep the old one.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Label</td><td>What people see on the review form, and what the AI is told the field is. Make it clear ("Total amount", not "Amt").</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Type</td><td>Text, Number, Money (R), Date or Yes / no. Codes and registrations are always Text, even when they look like numbers.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Unit</td><td>Optional: km, l, kg, R/l. Shown to the AI and on the form.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Core column</td><td>Puts the value in a real column on the slip for lists and reports (from 5a2b). Each one at most once per type.</td></tr>
                    <tr><td>Required</td><td>The field should be on every slip of this type; the AI is told to look carefully, and later a missing value flags the slip.</td></tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="mb-1">Core columns and the field types they accept:</p>
        <ul class="small">
            <li><strong>Amount</strong> (Money or Number): the total paid.</li>
            <li><strong>Quantity</strong> (Number): litres on a fuel slip, net mass on a weighbridge ticket, tonnes on a load.</li>
            <li><strong>Odometer</strong> (Number): the kilometre reading.</li>
            <li><strong>Reference no</strong> (Text): the slip, ticket or invoice number used to find doubles.</li>
            <li><strong>Vendor</strong> (Text): the seller, station or weighbridge site.</li>
            <li><strong>Slip date</strong> (Date): the date printed on the document; lists and reports use it.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Use <strong>Add field</strong> for a new row and the bin button to remove one. Empty rows are ignored when you save.</p>

        <h2 id="scope" class="h4 mt-5">5. Global and selected companies</h2>
        <p>Every entry has a scope:</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Global:</strong> for every company. Only one Global entry per key.</li>
            <li><strong>Selected companies:</strong> only for the companies you tick.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>For each key, a company gets <strong>one</strong> entry:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>an entry for selected companies that includes it and is on, otherwise</li>
            <li>the Global entry, if it is on, otherwise</li>
            <li>nothing (that type does not exist for the company).</li>
        </ol>
        <div class="tl-card small mb-3">
            <div class="fw-semibold mb-1">Example</div>
            Global <code>fuel</code> is for everyone. You make a <code>fuel</code> version for selected companies and tick ABC Transport.
            ABC Transport now gets only that version; every other company still gets the Global one.
            You also make a new type <code>loads</code> for ABC Transport only: it is an extra type for ABC, and nobody else sees it.
        </div>
        <ul>
            <li>A company can be in only <strong>one</strong> active selected-companies entry per key, so it never gets two fuel types. Untick it on the other entry or switch that one off first.</li>
            <li>Switching off a company's own version makes that company fall back to the Global entry.</li>
            <li>The Global system and detection prompts can be edited but never switched off or moved off Global, or no slip could be read. Give a company its own version instead.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="versions" class="h4 mt-5">6. Versions, History and Restore</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>Every <strong>Save as new version</strong> keeps a full copy (name, description, fields, prompt, scope, companies, on/off). Nothing is ever overwritten or deleted.</li>
            <li><strong>History</strong> (button on the entry, or the version number in the list) shows every version, newest first. Tap one to see it.</li>
            <li><strong>Restore this version</strong> saves the old content as a <em>new</em> version: restoring v1 when you are on v2 makes v3. The same checks as a normal save apply, so a restore can be refused if, for example, a ticked company now has another version.</li>
            <li>Every save and restore is in the <strong>Audit log</strong> (PromptCreated, PromptUpdated, PromptRestored).</li>
            <li>From 5a2b each slip records which versions read it, so you can compare before and after a change.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="effective" class="h4 mt-5">7. Effective for company</h2>
        <p>On the AI prompts page, pick a company and tap <strong>Show</strong>. You see the system prompt, the detection prompt with <code>{types}</code> filled in, and each capture type with <code>{fields}</code> filled in, exactly as the AI will get them for that company. Each one says <em>Global</em> or <em>For selected companies</em>. Use it after every scope change.</p>

        <h2 id="tasks" class="h4 mt-5">8. Common jobs, step by step</h2>
        <div class="tl-card mb-3" id="paste">
            <h3 class="h6">Make or improve an entry with Claude (copy and paste)</h3>
            <ol class="small mb-0">
                <li><strong>New type:</strong> ask Claude in the TransLog chat for "a TransLog prompt block for …" (for example loads). Copy its answer.</li>
                <li><strong>Improve one:</strong> open the entry, fold out <strong>Copy</strong> and tap Copy (or Share). Paste it to Claude with what goes wrong ("it mixes up the ticket and order number"), and copy its answer.</li>
                <li>Open the entry (or <strong>New type</strong>), fold out <strong>Paste</strong>, paste the answer and tap <strong>Fill in</strong>. Name, description, fields and prompt are filled; text around the block (a chat message) is ignored.</li>
                <li>Check the form, choose the scope, and tap <strong>Save as new version</strong>. If it reads worse, restore the old version in History.</li>
            </ol>
            <p class="small text-body-secondary mt-2 mb-0">Plain text that is not a block only replaces the prompt. Scope, companies and on/off are never in a block: they stay as set on the page.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="tl-card mb-3">
            <h3 class="h6">Improve a hint for everyone</h3>
            <ol class="small mb-0">
                <li>AI ▾ › AI prompts › tap the type (for example Fuel slip).</li>
                <li>Change the hints, or a field label.</li>
                <li><strong>Save as new version</strong>. If it reads worse, open History and restore the old version.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
        <div class="tl-card mb-3">
            <h3 class="h6">Give one company its own version of a type</h3>
            <ol class="small mb-0">
                <li>Open the Global entry (for example Weighbridge ticket).</li>
                <li>Tap <strong>Version for selected companies</strong>: a copy opens.</li>
                <li>Tick the company, change what it needs, Save.</li>
                <li>Check with <strong>Effective for company</strong>.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
        <div class="tl-card mb-3">
            <h3 class="h6">Add a new type (for example loads)</h3>
            <ol class="small mb-0">
                <li>AI prompts › <strong>New type</strong>.</li>
                <li>Name "Load / proof of delivery", key <code>loads</code>.</li>
                <li>Description: when a photo is this type ("A signed delivery note or POD for a load: client, load number, tonnes delivered").</li>
                <li>Fields, for example "Load number" (Text; under More: core Reference no, required), "Tonnes delivered" (Number, unit t, core Quantity), "Delivery date" (Date, core Slip date), "Client" (Text, core Vendor).</li>
                <li>Hints in plain words in the prompt, then choose Global or tick the companies, and Save. Easier still: paste a block from Claude (above).</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
        <div class="tl-card mb-3">
            <h3 class="h6">Stop using a type</h3>
            <p class="small mb-0">Open it, switch <strong>On</strong> off and Save. It is left out of detection and reading for every company it was for; old slips keep it. Switch it on again any time.</p>
        </div>

        <h2 id="refused" class="h4 mt-5">9. Why a save is refused</h2>
        <p>Nothing is saved when any of these is true; the reasons show in red at the top of the form.</p>
        <ul class="small">
            <li>The key is not lower case letters, digits and _ (2 to 40), or it is "system", "detect" or "other" on a type. (Leave it empty and one is made from the name.)</li>
            <li>There is already a Global entry with that key.</li>
            <li>A ticked company already has an active selected-companies entry with that key.</li>
            <li>Selected companies is chosen but no company is ticked.</li>
            <li>A type has no description or no fields; a field key is used twice or badly written; a field has no label; a core column is used twice or does not fit the field's type.</li>
            <li>You tried to switch off, or move off Global, the Global system or detection prompt.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="tips" class="h4 mt-5">10. Writing good prompts</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Hints beat rules.</strong> Say where things are on the slip: "Vehicle is often the pump id; the registration is under Notes".</li>
            <li><strong>Keep separate numbers separate.</strong> Two fields with two hints ("reference_no and slip_number are different numbers") stop the AI copying one into the other.</li>
            <li><strong>Never ask the AI to work things out</strong> (VAT, net mass, litres × price). TransLog checks the sums itself (Part 5c); the AI must only read.</li>
            <li><strong>Codes stay text.</strong> The AI mixes up S and 8, O and 0. The system prompt warns it, and TransLog's checks and the reviewer catch the rest; a confident "high" is not proof.</li>
            <li><strong>Descriptions should differ.</strong> If two types sound alike, step 1 will mix them up. Say what makes each one different ("not a fuel slip").</li>
            <li><strong>Change one thing at a time</strong> and test it on a few real slips (<a href="/sysadmin/aitest">AI test</a> page). If it gets worse, restore.</li>
            <li><strong>Keep it short.</strong> Every word is paid for on every slip; the system prompt and photo are cached, the rest is not.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="providers" class="h4 mt-5">11. AI providers and API keys</h2>
        <p>AI ▾ › <strong>AI providers</strong> lists the companies whose AI reads the slips. <strong>DeepSeek</strong> is there from the start (model <code>deepseek-flash</code>, the DeepSeek model that reads photos) but has no key until you type one.</p>
        <div class="tl-card mb-3">
            <h3 class="h6">Add the DeepSeek key</h3>
            <ol class="small mb-0">
                <li>On platform.deepseek.com, create an API key (it starts with <code>sk-</code>) and copy it.</li>
                <li>AI ▾ › AI providers › <strong>DeepSeek</strong>. Paste it into <strong>API key</strong> and Save. The page then shows "set ••••" with its last 4 characters.</li>
                <li>Tap <strong>Test</strong>: "The key works and deepseek-flash is available" means all is well. The test only asks DeepSeek for its model list, so it costs nothing.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
        <ul>
            <li>The key is stored <strong>encrypted</strong> and never shown again, not even to you. To change it, type the new one; to remove it, tick <em>Clear the stored key</em>.</li>
            <li>The encryption uses the same server keys as the login (<code>App_Data/keys</code>). If that folder is lost (a new server without a backup), the stored key cannot be read: Test then says so and you type it again. Keep <code>App_Data/keys</code> in the backups.</li>
            <li>A key in the server settings (<code>Ai:Providers:deepseek:ApiKey</code>) wins over a typed one; the page then says "from server settings".</li>
            <li>Only providers that are <strong>on and have a key</strong> can be chosen. The global provider, or one a company uses, cannot be switched off until you choose another.</li>
            <li>A company can have its own provider on its company page (SysAdmin › Companies › the company › <strong>AI provider</strong>). "Use global" follows Platform settings.</li>
            <li>For now only DeepSeek's own API is built. OpenRouter and other providers can be added later as one more kind.</li>
            <li>Every change is in the Audit log (AiProviderCreated, AiProviderUpdated, CompanyAiProviderChanged); the key itself is never written there.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="prices" class="h4 mt-5">12. Prices and cost</h2>
        <p>AI providers › the provider › <strong>Prices</strong>. Prices are US dollars per 1 million tokens (a token is a short piece of text; a photo counts as several hundred).</p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Input</strong> is what TransLog sends, <strong>Cached</strong> the part the provider already has from the first call (much cheaper), <strong>Output</strong> the answer.</li>
            <li>DeepSeek is cheaper <strong>off-peak</strong>. Peak is 01:00-04:00 and 06:00-10:00 UTC on weekdays, which is 03:00-06:00 and 08:00-12:00 South African time. Leave the off-peak boxes empty when a price is the same all day.</li>
            <li>When the provider changes its prices, <strong>add a new row</strong> with the date the change starts. Rows are never edited: the newest row on or before a slip's reading time counts, so old slips keep the cost of their time.</li>
            <li><strong>Typical slip</strong> shows what one slip costs with that price (two calls, about 2 400 tokens in, 600 out): about R0.01 to R0.02.</li>
            <li>Costs are estimates from the token counts the AI reports, not the provider's bill. Compare with the provider's usage page now and then. Chinese public holidays are counted as peak, so the estimate is a little high, never low.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="settings" class="h4 mt-5">13. Platform settings</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Global AI provider:</strong> reads every company's slips unless the company has its own.</li>
            <li><strong>Rand per US dollar:</strong> turns the dollar costs into rand. Update it by hand now and then (a "Get rate" button comes later). Each slip keeps the rate used when it was read.</li>
            <li><strong>AI auto-rotate:</strong> keep it off. It needs an image library on the server, which comes in a later part.</li>
            <li>Every change is in the Audit log with the old and new value (PlatformSettingChanged).</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="aitest" class="h4 mt-5">14. AI test</h2>
        <p>AI ▾ › <strong>AI test</strong> reads one photo exactly as a slip from the chosen company would be read, and shows everything. <strong>Nothing is stored</strong>: not the photo, not the answer. Each run costs about R0.01 to R0.02 and is written to the Audit log (AiTestRun).</p>
        <div class="tl-card mb-3">
            <h3 class="h6">Try a slip</h3>
            <ol class="small mb-0">
                <li>Choose the <strong>company</strong>: its prompts are used (global ones plus any for selected companies, as on Effective for company).</li>
                <li>Leave <strong>AI provider</strong> on "The company's provider", or pick another one to compare.</li>
                <li>Choose a <strong>photo</strong>. Keep <em>Shrink like the phone</em> ticked to test what a driver's phone really sends. Turn it with the arrows if it is sideways.</li>
                <li>Tap <strong>Read it</strong> and wait 10 to 25 seconds.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
        <ul>
            <li>The top box says how it ended and what the live app would do with the slip: <strong>Read</strong> (to Being checked), <strong>Type not sure</strong> (step 1 said "other", a type the company does not have, or low confidence; step 2 is not run), <strong>Could not be read</strong> (a bad answer twice), <strong>Provider problem</strong> (no answer, key refused, no balance: tried again later) or <strong>No provider</strong>.</li>
            <li><strong>Steps</strong> shows upload, detect, read and delete with time, tokens and cost. "tried twice" means the first answer was empty, cut off or not json (both tries are paid for). The read step's input is mostly <em>cached</em>: the system prompt and photo come from step 1.</li>
            <li><strong>Fields read</strong> lists every field of the type with the AI's value and confidence; <strong>Core columns</strong> shows what would go into the slip date, amount, quantity, odometer, reference and vendor columns.</li>
            <li><strong>Answers and prompts</strong> holds both raw answers and the three prompts exactly as sent, each with Copy. Paste them into a Claude chat to improve a prompt, then paste the new prompt block on the prompt's edit page.</li>
            <li>After changing a prompt, run the same photo again: the versions used are shown under the result.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="later" class="h4 mt-5">15. What comes later</h2>
        <ul class="small">
            <li><strong>5a2b2:</strong> every slip that drivers and the office send is read automatically in the background (started on upload, checked every 30 s and at startup), with the type, slip date, values, prompt versions and cost in rand stored on the slip; an AI reads page lists recent reads.</li>
            <li><strong>5d:</strong> "Check provider" finds new prices on the provider's own site for you to approve, and "Get rate" fetches the dollar rate.</li>
            <li><strong>Part 7:</strong> the full type editor (choice lists, vehicle and driver pickers), companies' own types, and photo checks such as "Is an open diesel cap visible?".</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</section>
